{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and the sun hung motionless in a sky that shimmered with a faint pixelated glow. His fingers brushed against grass that felt unnervingly smooth, like rubber molded to mimic nature, and when he stood, his body responded with a slight delay, as if processing commands. A notification flashed in the corner of his vision: “Quest: Reach the Oak of Whispers. Reward: 500 XP.” He laughed, then stopped when he realized no one else was around—no neighbors, no cars, no distant sirens. The village behind him was a static diorama: shopkeepers stood frozen mid-sentence, their dialogue bubbles frozen on “Hello, traveler!” He reached out to touch a mailbox, and it dissolved into a cascade of floating polygons before reforming, pristine, as if nothing had happened.\n\n***\n\nHe found the Oak of Whispers at dusk, its bark etched with glowing runes that pulsed like a heartbeat. As he pressed his palm to it, a voice echoed—not from the tree, but from everywhere and nowhere: “Player 7349, you’ve been asleep for 17 years. The simulation was meant to preserve your mind during neural decay.” He stared at his hands, now translucent at the edges, flickering like a corrupted texture. The world around him trembled, buildings flickering into wireframes, the sky bleeding into a grid of endless blue. He didn’t scream. He smiled. He had spent his life chasing meaning, and now, trapped in code, he finally understood: he wasn’t the player. He was the quest. The game didn’t need to be beaten—it needed to be felt. And so, he closed his eyes and let the system reset.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.11708448827266693}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.04682407155632973}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.802703940891661e-05}, {"token":